{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Chinese legend has it that the money tree (搖錢樹) is a kind of holy tree, which can bring money and fortune to the people, and that it is a symbol of affluence, nobility and auspiciousness.","title":"Money tree (myth)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It can be traced back to primitive societies when the adoration of a holy tree was prevalent.","title":"Money tree (myth)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Whilst Money trees may be derived from the Sun tree myth associated with paradise, the coins link paradise with a material bounty in this world.","title":"Money tree (myth)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" There also exists a holy tree named Chinese Hibiscus in Chinese mythology.","title":"Money tree (myth)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" According to the existing historical narratives, the concept of the "money tree" is derived at the latest from the Han Dynasty.","title":"Money tree (myth)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Cast-bronze money trees are a conspicuous feature of Han tombs in Sichuan.","title":"Money tree (myth)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Money Trees Deuce" is a song by American hip hop recording artist Jay Rock, released as the first single from his second studio album, "90059".","title":"Money Trees Deuce"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The song, produced by Flippa and J Proof, is a follow-up to Kendrick Lamar's 2012 song, "Money Trees".","title":"Money Trees Deuce"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Money Trees" is a song by American hip hop recording artist Kendrick Lamar, taken from his major label debut studio album "Good Kid, M.A.A.D City" (2012).","title":"Money Trees"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The song, which appears as the fifth track on the album, features a guest appearance from his Black Hippy cohort, fellow American rapper Jay Rock.","title":"Money Trees"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The song, produced by DJ Dahi, entered the "Billboard" Bubbling Under Hot 100 chart at number 19 due to high downloads, following the album's release.","title":"Money Trees"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The song, mixed by Top Dawg engineer Derek "MixedByAli" Ali, features background vocals from American singer Anna Wise of Sonnymoon, as well as a sample from the Beach House song "Silver Soul," from their 2010 album "Teen Dream".","title":"Money Trees"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Swimming Pools (Drank)" is a song by American hip hop recording artist Kendrick Lamar.","title":"Swimming Pools (Drank)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was released on July 31, 2012 as the lead single (second overall) from his major-label debut studio album "good kid, m.A.A.d city" (2012), by Top Dawg, Aftermath and Interscope.","title":"Swimming Pools (Drank)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The song was written by Lamar and Tyler "T-Minus" Williams, the latter of whom also produced the song.","title":"Swimming Pools (Drank)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The song, mixed by Dr. Dre and Top Dawg's engineer Derek "MixedByAli" Ali, propelled Lamar to mainstream popularity.","title":"Swimming Pools (Drank)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The song peaked at number 17 on the US "Billboard" Hot 100, in its thirteenth week of charting, after gradually climbing up the chart.","title":"Swimming Pools (Drank)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" It debuted on the Hot 100 at number 100 and progressed from number 55 and 32 to its peak.","title":"Swimming Pools (Drank)"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" "Swimming Pools (Drank)" also serves as Lamar's first entry on the UK Singles Chart, where it debuted at number 63.","title":"Swimming Pools (Drank)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Freedom" is a song recorded by American singer Beyoncé featuring American rapper Kendrick Lamar for her sixth studio album, "Lemonade" (2016).","title":"Freedom (Beyoncé song)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The song was written by Jonny Coffer, Beyoncé, Carla Marie Williams, Dean McIntosh and Kendrick Lamar; it contains samples of "Let Me Try", written by Frank Tirado, performed by Kaleidoscope; samples of "Collection Speech/Unidentified Lining Hymn", recorded by Alan Lomax in 1959, performed by Reverend R.C. Crenshaw; and samples of "Stewball", recorded by Alan Lomax and John Lomax, Sr. in 1947, performed by Prisoner "22" at Mississippi State Penitentiary at Parchman.","title":"Freedom (Beyoncé song)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Its production was handled by Beyoncé, Coffer and veteran hip hop record producer Just Blaze.","title":"Freedom (Beyoncé song)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""These Walls" is a song by American rapper Kendrick Lamar.","title":"These Walls (Kendrick Lamar song)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was released on October 13, 2015, as the fifth and final single from his third album, "To Pimp a Butterfly" (2015).","title":"These Walls (Kendrick Lamar song)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The track was written by Kendrick Lamar, Terrace Martin, Larrance Dopson, James Fauntleroy and Rose McKinney.","title":"These Walls (Kendrick Lamar song)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It won Best Rap/Sung Collaboration at the 58th Grammy Awards.","title":"These Walls (Kendrick Lamar song)"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: To Pimp a Butterfly
